{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>How long will you refuse?</b> According to what I raised in the verse ״וַיַּגִּידוּ לְמֹשֶׁה״ — that Moses committed no wrong in not telling them this matter beforehand — if so, what place is there to include him with the refrainers to keep the commandments of HaShem? And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Bava Kama 92a) said: בַּהֲדֵי הוּצָא לָקֵי כַּרְבָא — 'with the swarm he was stricken with the many,' and their words of blessed memory are justified, for he caused no stumbling. And it appears to me that in any event the verse would not include him with the rest unless HaShem had upon him even a hair's breadth of indignation. And I looked into Moses' statement near this verse, where he said to Israel ״רְאוּ״ etc., and one needs to know when this prophecy was spoken to Moses: if it was said after He said to him ״עַד אָנָה מֵאַנְתֶּם״ — the question cries out to our God: why did He not command him before commanding Israel ״אַל יֵצֵא אִישׁ״ etc.? And if it was said to him beforehand — how did Moses not say it to Israel?" "<b>Indeed</b>, this is the stumble of the man of God, for the cleverness he employed with his understanding of HaShem's word — in that [HaShem] did not say to him ״דַּבֵּר״ etc. or ״לֵאמֹר״ — he should have said it to Israel, yet he said to them nothing but ״הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳״ etc., and did not tell them the outcome of this deed that HaShem did — the double portion of bread — which was to give them the Sabbath by way of providing for them through it, in the manner we explained in the verse (Exodus 16:22) ״וַיָּבֹאוּ כָּל נְשִׂיאֵי הָעֵדָה״ etc. And from this it resulted that they went out on the holy Sabbath day from among the people to gather, for they reasoned: since he did not give them an explicit warning but merely explained the matter ״הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר״ etc., they did not take it to heart and went out to gather and were not concerned about this — until afterward, when he said to them ״אַל יֵצֵא״ etc.; then the verse says ״וַיִּשְׁבְּתוּ הָעָם״ etc. — from this you learn that it was not so beforehand. Therefore HaShem included him and said ״עַד אָנָה מֵאַנְתֶּם״. And therefore when Moses saw that HaShem was indignant with him, he understood the matter and immediately said ״רְאוּ כִּי ה׳״ etc. — and these are the words that were spoken to him at the beginning of HaShem's word concerning this matter (Exodus 16:5) ״וְהֵכִינוּ אֵת אֲשֶׁר יָבִיאוּ״, as I explained there. And from here you learn that on account of his withholding this statement from Israel, HaShem included him with the refrainers — for he should have told Israel the intent of HaShem just as he grasped it with his own understanding, as we explained in the verse ״וַיַּגִּידוּ לְמֹשֶׁה״. And I maintain that in this matter there is not enough to chastise him with a great rod of discipline, saying to him ״עַד אָנָה מֵאַנְתֶּם״ — for he was not commanded in particular and did not transgress even a hair's breadth, except that he should have informed Israel of what he grasped with supernal understanding so they would not err by going out as they did err; and there is no punishment in this. Therefore they of blessed memory said (Bava Kama 92a): בַּהֲדֵי הוּצָא לָקֵי כַּרְבָא — a reproof that is not fitting for him." "<b>My commandments and my teachings.</b> For we find of them that they transgressed two commandments: one, that He said to them ״אַל יוֹתֵר מִמֶּנּוּ״ and they left over; and the second, that He said to them ״תִּלְקְטוּהוּ וּבַיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי לֹא יִהְיֶה בּוֹ״ the gathering, and they transgressed and went out to gather. Therefore He said ״עַד אָנָה מֵאַנְתֶּם לִשְׁמֹר מִצְוֹתַי״. And His saying <b>וְתוֹרֹתָי</b> — they too are two teachings: one, that HaShem told them they should gather an omer per head and they gathered more, as they of blessed memory expounded (Mekhilta) on the verse ״וְלֹא הֶעְדִּיף הַמַּרְבֶּה״; and in the matter of the Sabbath too, what He gave them as a double portion on Friday did not suffice them and they went out to gather more than an omer per head; and the second, that they did not believe the word of HaShem that on the Sabbath day none would be there, and they went out etc. — and you have no one who transgresses a teaching such as this who is not one who does not believe in the Torah of Moses His servant."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :register {:flags {0 ["God"], 1 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase בַּהֲדֵי הוּצָא לָקֵי כַּרְבָא (b. Bava Kama 92a) is left untranslated as the Or HaChaim references it as a known Rabbinic idiom; it means roughly 'he who goes out with the swarm is struck with the many,' i.e., one who is counted among wrongdoers suffers with them.", 1 "The phrase קֻשְׁיָא לֵאלֹהֵינוּ (lit. 'a difficulty to our God') is an idiomatic intensifier meaning the question is very strong; rendered literally to preserve the Or HaChaim's rhetorical force."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:20.271793220Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}